  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 247 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Low-amplitude 850-700mb trough departs ewd this evening \nallowing stratiform rain to taper off quickly. Clearing line has \nalready reached the immediate St. Lawrence Valley as of 18 UTC. \nLingering light rain across central/ern VT should exit east of the \nCT River Valley by 2030 UTC. With wet low-level/soil conditions, it \nappears that patchy dense fog will develop in spots after sunset. \nNAM/HRRR/RRFS model soundings all show a better surge of low-level \nCAA during the pre-dawn hours, and better mixing may yield more low \nstratus as opposed to fog as we head towards daybreak. Either way, \nshould see a combination of fog/low clouds helping to keep overnight \nlows mostly in the low-mid 40s areawide. A few 30s are possible in \nthe nrn Adirondacks especially around KSLK. Winds will also \npick up on Lake Champlain out of the north late (see Marine \nsection below). \n\nLarge area of high pressure building esewd from northern Ontario \nwill then be our controlling weather feature Mon-Thu. Low clouds \ngradually clear on Monday with increasing large-scale subsidence, \nleading toward crisp fall like conditions through the remainder of \nthe short term forecast period with PoPs NIL. Looking for highs in \nthe upper 50s to lower 60s on Monday, then generally low-mid 60s for \nTuesday and Wednesday. Overnight lows in the 30s to lower 40s with \ngood radiational cooling conditions expected. Look for some patchy \nfrost away from the Champlain Valley Monday and Tuesday night...and \nlocally widespread frost in the northern Adirondacks and Vermont's \nNortheast Kingdom.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Guidance is in good agreement through late week with \nregards to large high pres anchored over southern Canada and keeping \nour fa dry. However, a large spread begins to develop Friday into \nnext weekend with regards to evolution and track of coastal low \npres, and potential impacts to our cwa. Attm WPC continues to keep \nchc pops into the fcst for late Friday into next weekend, with \nhighest probabilities acrs central/eastern VT and <10% near the \nInternational Border. Initially, deep dry layer in place with pw \nvalues btwn 0.20 to 0.40\" wl produce sunny conditions on Thurs with \nseasonably cool temps. Highs generally in the upper 50s to mid/upper \n60s Thurs/Friday with lows mid 30s to mid 40s. It should be noted, \nthe lows fcsted by WPC, especially Weds and Thurs nights are too \nwarm, given MEX at SLK is 24F and 28F, while WPC has 34F and 33F. \nSimilar scenario is noted acrs central/northern and eastern VT, \nwhere daily frost advisories will probably be required, given the \nfavorable setup for cool night time. As clouds and moisture slowly \nadvect back into our cwa by next week, daily low temps warm back \ninto the 40s, but cooler daytime highs with northerly flow are \npossible by next weekend. The bottom line is we are not expecting \nany widespread hazardous or significant weather late week into next \nweekend attm.",
